cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below. It is a research prototype: classical, deterministic, standard-library Python, with illustrative and uncalibrated thresholds."
type: software
title: "identity-runtime: governed, reversible avatar personalities with leased capabilities"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-10-18"
license: AGPL-3.0-only
repository-code: "https://github.com/sparkainlp-x/identity-runtime"
url: "https://sparkainlp-x.github.io/identity-runtime/"
authors:
  - family-names: "Brisson"
    given-names: "Jean-François"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0000-9778-5374"
    affiliation: "Spark AI NLP, Fredericton, New Brunswick, Canada"
abstract: >-
  A standard-library Python research prototype that treats an avatar
  personality as a versioned identity state rather than a file. Uploaded
  manifests are checked against a schema, compiled against a runtime-side capability
  registry and screened by a deterministic shadow simulation, then wait for
  human approval (two distinct approvers for tool and safety policy) before
  they commit. Capabilities are short, revocable leases granted by a person;
  actions are denied, held for a human or authorised with a proof object
  recorded in a fail-closed, hash-chained ledger. Committed versions are kept
  by checksum and can be rolled back. A fixed hierarchy of 61,440 threshold
  channels ("OES-61440") acts as a transparent tripwire; it is deterministic,
  not learned, uncalibrated, and not a safety guarantee. Text screening can be
  bypassed by paraphrase, nothing is executed or sandboxed, and approvals use
  optional shared-secret HMAC tokens, not public-key signatures. Example
  personalities include a fictional educational character inspired by Nikola
  Tesla (1856-1943), not affiliated with Tesla, Inc., and the Sparky the
  Beaver mascot.
keywords:
  - research-software
  - avatar-governance
  - capability-leases
  - human-in-the-loop
  - audit-ledger
  - hash-chain
  - rollback
  - personality-manifest
